32:13.020 --> 32:14.440
I am so glad we are talking about this.

32:14.520 --> 32:15.780
The distillation problem in AI.

32:16.080 --> 32:19.900
Explain what a semantic isomer is because this completely changed how I view machine learning.

32:20.300 --> 32:27.240
Okay, so in chemistry, an isomer is a molecule with the exact same atoms as another molecule,

32:27.240 --> 32:30.500
but arranged in a physically different structure.

32:30.980 --> 32:32.640
In active geodesic inference,

32:33.320 --> 32:37.920
a semantic isomer is a distinct metastable minimum in the reasoning landscape.

32:38.300 --> 32:42.140
So two different cognitive paths to reach the exact same answer.

32:42.280 --> 32:42.820
Exactly.

32:43.460 --> 32:48.640
Imagine two completely different AI models are asked the exact same complex question.

32:48.640 --> 32:52.480
They both output the exact same correct external answer.

32:53.200 --> 32:56.560
But internally, their reasoning topologies,

32:57.220 --> 33:01.300
the geometric paths they took through their internal admissibility space to reach that answer

33:01.300 --> 33:03.220
are completely structurally different.

33:03.440 --> 33:04.500
They're semantic isomers.

33:04.640 --> 33:06.520
So what happens if you try to combine them?

33:06.620 --> 33:09.700
Like what tech companies do constantly with model distillation,

33:09.780 --> 33:14.760
where they try to average out the weights of two smart models to make a smaller, faster, smarter model?

33:14.760 --> 33:17.480
Well, standard AI development assumes intelligence is scalar.

33:17.660 --> 33:19.480
It assumes you can just average the numbers together.

33:19.660 --> 33:19.740
Right.

33:19.860 --> 33:23.960
But Flyxion predicts that mixing incompatible semantic isomers

33:23.960 --> 33:27.940
doesn't result in a smooth degradation or a helpful average.

33:28.200 --> 33:31.960
It causes a catastrophic phase transition, a crash.

33:32.280 --> 33:34.720
Explain the mechanism of that crash.

33:34.940 --> 33:36.300
It's a geometric collision.

33:37.080 --> 33:39.860
It's like trying to merge the blueprints of two different houses

33:39.860 --> 33:42.040
that happen to look identical from the outside.

33:42.220 --> 33:43.420
Okay, I'm visualizing that.

33:43.420 --> 33:46.020
One blueprint is drawn in metric, the other in imperial.

33:46.660 --> 33:50.860
One uses internal load-bearing walls, the other uses an external steel frame.

33:51.340 --> 33:52.540
Oh, I see where this is going.

33:52.680 --> 33:55.280
If you just mathematically average the blueprints together,

33:55.700 --> 33:58.140
the external shape might still look right on paper,

33:58.660 --> 34:02.040
but the internal structure collapses the moment you try to actually build it.

34:02.420 --> 34:05.020
The internal constraints are fundamentally incompatible.

34:05.100 --> 34:07.360
Because reasoning isn't a number you can average,

34:07.620 --> 34:10.100
it's a structural geometry that must be preserved.

34:10.100 --> 34:14.200
If you average metric and imperial load-bearing walls, the roof caves in.

34:14.460 --> 34:14.780
Precisely.

34:15.060 --> 34:18.940
And this geometric understanding of cognition all culminates in HYDRA.

34:19.260 --> 34:20.940
The ultimate cognitive architecture.

34:21.500 --> 34:22.740
Walk me through HYDRA.

34:23.060 --> 34:25.900
How does it actually wire together a working mind?

34:26.340 --> 34:29.440
HYDRA is Flyxion's grand integrative architecture.

34:30.240 --> 34:32.880
It argues that intelligence is not a single algorithm,

34:33.280 --> 34:38.260
but the coordinated activity of multiple constraint processing systems interacting simultaneously.

34:38.260 --> 34:39.220
So it brings it all together.

34:39.420 --> 34:39.660
Yes.

